      "Why Urban Nighttime Heat Lingers Long After Sunset—and How Pavement Chemistry Shapes It",
      "Public warnings about metropolitan heatwaves understandably emphasize afternoon peak temperatures, when sunlit plazas shimmer above thirty-eight degrees Celsius. Epidemiologists tracking hospital admissions and excess summer mortality, however, point out that human cardiovascular strain depends just as heavily on what happens between midnight and five in the morning. When indoor and outdoor temperatures refuse to drop below twenty-six or twenty-eight degrees overnight, blood vessels cannot dilate efficiently to shed core body heat during sleep, depriving older adults and outdoor workers of physiological recovery before the next afternoon's thermal assault.\n\nUrban climatologists trace stubborn nocturnal warmth to the high \"thermal admittance\" and canyon geometry of conventional building materials. Dense dark asphalt roadways, poured concrete parking decks, and solid masonry facades absorb shortwave solar radiation throughout the afternoon and store megajoules of heat deep beneath their surfaces, whereas rural pastures and tree leaves cool swiftly after dusk. Once the sun sets, cities release that banked heat upward as longwave infrared radiation—only for narrow street canyons and overhanging building walls to intercept outgoing rays and bounce them back toward the pavement instead of letting heat escape into the night sky, compounded by waste heat exhausted continuously from millions of air-conditioning condensers. Standard heat-mitigation coatings that merely paint dark streets bright white reflect midday glare onto pedestrians and building windows without necessarily accelerating after-dark cooling if underlying slabs remain thick and heat-retentive. Conventional urban surfaces thus (23).\n\nCooling cities after sunset requires matching material porosity, albedo, and waste-heat engineering rather than relying on reflective paint alone. Pavement researchers are testing permeable open-graded concretes and water-retaining ceramic blocks irrigated with harvested greywater that cool streets via afternoon evaporation and store far less sensible heat by nightfall, alongside \"radiative-cooling\" roof membranes engineered to emit thermal energy precisely within the eight-to-thirteen-micrometer atmospheric window that passes straight through humid air into outer space. District-cooling networks that reject air-conditioning heat into deep river loops or geothermal boreholes rather than hot apartment courtyards further trim nocturnal ambient temperatures by one to two degrees. (24), municipalities can restore the nighttime temperature drop human physiology requires. Protecting heat-stressed cities ultimately obliges urban designers to measure success not only at solar noon, but by (25).",
      "都市の猛暑に関する公的な注意喚起が、日なたの広場が摂氏38度を超えて陽炎の立つ午後の最高気温を強調するのは当然のことだ。しかし入院件数や夏季の超過死亡を追跡する疫学者は、人間の心血管系にかかる負担は深夜0時から早朝5時までの気温推移にも同程度に左右されると指摘する。屋内外の気温が一晩中26〜28度を下回らない熱帯夜が続くと、睡眠中に血管を効率よく拡張させて深部体温を逃がすことができず、高齢者や屋外労働者は翌午後の熱ストレスが襲う前に生理的回復を果たせなくなるのだ。都市気候学者はこのしつこい夜間高温の原因を、従来の建材が持つ高い「熱アドミタンス（蓄熱・熱浸透特性）」と街路の幾何学構造に求める。高密度の黒いアスファルト道路やコンクリート駐車場、石造・れんが壁面は午後じゅう短波日射を吸収して内部深くに膨大な熱量を蓄える一方、農村の牧草地や木の葉は日没後に素早く冷える。日が沈むと都市は蓄えた熱を長波赤外線として上方へ放出し始めるが、狭い街路空間と迫り立つビル壁面が放射を遮って舗道側へ跳ね返すため夜空へ熱が抜けず、さらに何百万台ものエアコン室外機から昼夜吐き出される排熱が追い打ちをかける。しかも黒い路面をただ真っ白に塗るだけの単純な遮熱塗装は、真昼の照り返しで歩行者や窓際を眩しく暑くするうえ、下地スラブが分厚く蓄熱性が高ければ日没後の冷却を必ずしも早めない。従来の都市表面はこうして(23)のである。日没後の都市を冷やすには反射塗料頼みではなく多孔性・反射率・排熱処理を組み合わせる必要がある。再生中水を含ませて午後の気化熱で冷やし日没までの蓄熱自体を抑える保水性・透水性舗装や、湿った大気を突き抜けて宇宙空間へ直接抜ける波長8〜13マイクロメートルの「大気の窓」領域で熱放射する放射冷却屋根材、さらに空調排熱を中庭の空気ではなく河川水ループや地中熱井戸へ逃がす地域冷房網は夜間気温を1〜2度引き下げる。(24)、自治体は人間の身体機能が必要とする夜間の気温低下を取り戻せる。暑熱ストレスから都市を守るには、正午の気温だけでなく(25)によって対策の成否を測る視点が不可欠である。",
